Erlang-Lex: a table-driven lexer generator in Erlang.

Give it a list of rules (regular expression -> action); it builds a
DFA at run time (regexp -> NFA -> DFA -> minimized DFA -> table, as in
the Dragon book) and splits input into tokens by longest match, like
Unix lex/flex. If several rules match the same longest token, the rule
with the highest priority wins, then the earliest rule.

  1> lex:with([{regexp, "\\p{L}+", lex:item(word)},
               {regexp, "\\s+", lex:whitespace()}],
              <<"Grüße мир"/utf8>>, [{encoding, utf8}]).
  [{word,1,"Grüße"},{word,1,"мир"}]

Features:
- input as bytes, Latin-1, UTF-8, UTF-16 LE/BE or UTF-32 LE/BE; the
  lexer reads one byte at a time and rejects malformed or truncated
  input with a precise error
- named Unicode classes in Perl syntax (\p{L}, \p{Greek}, \p{sc=Grek},
  \p{InCyrillic}, \p{XID_Start}, \P{...}), \d \s \w, \x{1F600}
- POSIX classes ([[:alpha:]], [[:^digit:]])
- case-insensitive matching ((?i), (?i:...), option case_insensitive)
- Unicode 17.0.0 data, regenerated from the UCD with `make unicode-data`

Build and test:

  make
  make test

Documentation:
- doc/README.md   API, usage, examples, how it works, references,
                  limitations and future work
- doc/syntax.md   the rule and regular expression syntax
- src/lex_examples.erl  the documented examples (tested)

Building the table dynamically is fast enough for most uses (a few
milliseconds for a programming language lexer); use
lex:with(Rules, Input) or keep the result of lex:regexps_to_table/2.

TODO (see doc/README.md, "Limitations and future work", for more):
- refactor [lex gen; lex driver; lex codegen; tokenization library]
- improved error checking
  * better messages on overlapping matches
- improved matching
  * current default is to fail on no match, but tools like lex/flex instead
    emit/skip the current char as a default and continue
- lexing directly on binaries (now converted to a list of bytes)
- support for incremental lexing
  (basically return 'current state' and allow resume later)
- Unicode: normalization (canonical equivalence), grapheme clusters,
  full (multi-character) case folding, more properties, set operations
  in classes
- code generation
  * emit a table as a constant data structure, and the lex driver with it,
    and a suitable API
  * maybe: emit leex-style full code for the lexer using the table
    - worth checking which is faster and smaller
  * note: for Unicode, the table can be large (in particular for the
    little-endian encodings); byte equivalence classes or leex-style
    code may help
